  "rationale": "All four candidates are direct matches at the trait concept level (trait_efo = hypertrophic cardiomyopathy). Endpoint fidelity is strongest for PGS004910 and PGS004911 because phenotyping_reported is explicitly “Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy”, whereas PGS000778 uses a restricted endpoint (“Clinical events in individuals with a pathogenic or likely pathogenic sarcomeric variant”), making it less aligned to generic HCM risk. Between PGS004910 and PGS004911, both come from the same disease-focused publication family, use the same method (PRS-CS) with similarly genome-wide variant counts (~374k), the same covariate pattern (age, age^2, sex, PC1-10), and the same evaluation ancestry (EUR), so they form a coherent high-support cluster; within this sibling pair, PGS004911 is preferred because it reports higher full-model AUROC (0.8 vs 0.73) and higher R² (0.048 vs 0.031), and domain guidance indicates MTAG is a legitimate power-boosting enhancement rather than an endpoint mismatch. Main limitation: the evidence provided is largely full-model metrics (not explicitly PRS-only/incremental), so the degree to which performance reflects the PRS alone vs covariates cannot be fully resolved from the visible metadata, lowering confidence to Moderate."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 returned models are direct or near-direct matches to the target concept of nodular goiter: two UKB portability-framework scores for "Nontoxic multinodular goiter" (PGS001814, PGS002022) and three disease-focused multi-biobank scores for "Benign nodular goiter" (PGS005261-263), plus two case-control contrast scores "Thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter" (PGS005273-274) whose endpoints are not pure nodular-goiter risk.

PGS005262 is the best-supported direct-match candidate from the visible evidence because it is endpoint-aligned (benign nodular goiter) and comes from a coherent disease-focused multi-ancestry thyroid-disease study family (many contributing cohorts listed), while also using a methodologically strong genome-wide shrinkage approach (PRSCS) with very high variant count (1,085,173) and it has the strongest reported discrimination among the benign-nodular-goiter siblings (full_model AUROC 0.5933 vs 0.5854 and 0.5876). By contrast, the UKB portability-framework models are explicitly from a portability sweep and show only low partial-r values (~0.024–0.028), and the carcinoma-vs-benign scores are not directly measuring nodular goiter susceptibility.

Limitation: the top candidate’s covariates are listed as "Unknown" and the reported metric is full-model AUROC (domain knowledge notes full-model AUROC can be non-comparable and should not be over-weighted), so while PGS005262 is preferred, the evidence is not strong enough to call it unambiguously high-quality."}
